# reading 3 of kant-groundwork-1.md by claude-opus-5-5
logic	s5
atom	gw	the being has a good will
atom	other_unqual	something other than a good will is good without qualification
atom	talents_bad	the gifts of nature (talents of the mind, qualities of temperament) become extremely bad and mischievous
atom	fortune_pride	the gifts of fortune (power, riches, honour, health, happiness) inspire pride and often presumption
atom	pleased	the sight of the being enjoying unbroken prosperity gives pleasure to an impartial rational spectator
atom	worthy	the being is worthy of happiness
# S1: "nothing can possibly be conceived ... except a good will" read as: it is not possible that anything else is good without qualification
P1	S1	¬◇other_unqual
# S2: "may also become extremely bad" read as possibility, conditional on the will not being good
P2	S2	¬gw → ◇talents_bad
# S3 ("It is the same with the gifts of fortune") only introduces S4, which states the claim
P3	S4	¬gw → fortune_pride
# S5: "can never give pleasure" read as necessity
P4	S5	□(¬gw → ¬pleased)
# The step to S6 plainly relies on the spectator's pleasure tracking worthiness of happiness
A1	-	□(worthy → pleased)
# S6: "indispensable condition" read as necessity; "appears" hedges but the conclusion is drawn ("Thus")
C	S6	□(worthy → gw)	from P4 A1
